"lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Akitu</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">Akitu was the Mesopotamian New Year festival, celebrated most famously at Babylon in honour of the god Marduk.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/akkad"><span class="enc-name">Akkad</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Akkad was the capital city of the Akkadian Empire, founded or developed by Sargon in the twenty-fourth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/akkadian-empire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Akkadian Empire</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Akkadian Empire was the first known empire in Mesopotamia, founded by Sargon of Akkad around the twenty-fourth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-language"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/akkadian-language.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Akkadian language</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Akkadian is an extinct Semitic language of ancient Mesopotamia, written in cuneiform.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/akkar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/akkar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Akkar</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Akkar is the northernmost region of Lebanon, a fertile coastal plain and hill country on the border with Syria, now a governorate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/akmal-ikramov"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/akmal-ikramov.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Akmal Ikramov</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Akmal Ikramov was an Uzbek communist leader, first secretary of the Communist Party of the Uzbek SSR from 1929 to 1937.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/akova"><span class="enc-name">Akova</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Akova was a castle in the mountains of Arcadia in the central Peloponnese, which the French called Matagrifon.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dakovo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dakovo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Đakovo</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Đakovo is a town in the Slavonia region of eastern Croatia, seat of a Catholic bishopric since the 13th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/akropong"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/akropong.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Akropong</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Akropong is a town on the Akuapem ridge in south-eastern Ghana and the seat of the Okuapehene, paramount chief of the Akuapem state, since the 18th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/akrotiri-prehistoric-city"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/akrotiri-prehistoric-city.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Akrotiri (prehistoric city)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Akrotiri is a Bronze Age settlement on the island of Santorini (Thera) in the Aegean, buried by a volcanic eruption in the 17th or 16th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aksaray"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aksaray.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aksaray</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Aksaray is a city in central Anatolia, Turkey, at the foot of the volcano Hasan Dağı, and capital of the province of the same name.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aksehir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aksehir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Akşehir</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Akşehir is a town in Konya Province in central Turkey, at the foot of the Sultan Mountains, ancient Philomelium.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aksu"><span class="enc-name">Aksu</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Aksu is an oasis city in the Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region of China, on the northern rim of the Tarim Basin at the foot of the Tian Shan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-conquest-of-kush"><span class="enc-name">Aksumite conquest of Kush</span><span class="enc-type">Campaign · c. 350 CE</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aksumite conquest of Kush was the campaign led by the Aksumite king Ezana around 350 CE against the Kingdom of Kush and its centre at Meroë.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aksumite-empire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aksumite Empire</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aksumite Empire was a trading state centred on Aksum in the northern Ethiopian highlands and present-day Eritrea, flourishing from roughly the first to the seventh…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-invasion-of-kush"><span class="enc-name">Aksumite invasion of Kush</span><span class="enc-type">Campaign · c. 4th century CE</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aksumite invasion of Kush refers to military expeditions by the Aksumite Empire into the Kingdom of Kush in the c. 4th century CE, most notably under King Ezana.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aktekke-mosque"><span class="enc-name">Aktekke Mosque</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aktekke Mosque is a 14th-century mosque in Karaman, central Anatolia, built under the Karamanid ruler Alaeddin Bey on the site of an earlier dervish lodge.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aku-uka"><span class="enc-name">Aku Uka</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Aku Uka is the title of the sacred king of the Jukun people of the Benue valley in Nigeria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/akuapem-twi"><span class="enc-name">Akuapem Twi</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Akuapem Twi is a dialect of the Akan language spoken on the Akuapem ridge in south-eastern Ghana.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/akufuna-tatila"><span class="enc-name">Akufuna Tatila</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Akufuna Tatila was briefly Litunga, king of the Lozi of Barotseland, in 1884–1885.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/akusha"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/akusha.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Akusha</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Akusha is a village in the mountains of central Dagestan, Russia, and the historic centre of the Dargin people.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/akwamu"><span class="enc-name">Akwamu</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">Akwamu was an Akan state in the forest and coastal region of present-day Ghana that was powerful in the seventeenth and early eighteenth centuries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/akwesasne"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/akwesasne.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Akwesasne</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Akwesasne is a Mohawk community on the St Lawrence river that straddles the border between Canada and the United States, divided among Quebec, Ontario and New York State.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-ali"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-ali.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al Ali</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Al Ali are an Arab tribe of the Trucial Coast, centred on Umm al-Quwain in the United Arab Emirates.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-bu-falasah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-bu-falasah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al Bu Falasah</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Al Bu Falasah are a section of the Bani Yas tribal confederation of the southern Gulf coast.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-bu-said-dynasty"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-bu-said-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al Bu Said dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Al Bu Said dynasty has ruled Oman since the 1740s, when Ahmad ibn Said expelled the Persians and was elected imam in place of the Ya'aruba.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-fadl"><span class="enc-name">Al Fadl</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">Al Fadl was an Arab Bedouin tribe of the Syrian desert, descended from the Tayy and the Jarrahid family.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-hoceima"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-hoceima.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al Hoceima</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Al Hoceima is a port city on the Mediterranean coast of northern Morocco, in the Rif.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-jazeera"><span class="enc-name">Al Jazeera</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Al Jazeera is a television network based in Doha, launched in November 1996 with funding from the emir of Qatar, Hamad bin Khalifa Al Thani.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-madhkur"><span class="enc-name">Al Madhkur</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Al Madhkur were an Arab family settled on the Persian coast who ruled the port of Bushehr as sheikhs from the mid-18th century under Persian overlordship.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mahatta-fort"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-mahatta-fort.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al Mahatta Fort</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Al Mahatta Fort is a fortified rest house built in Sharjah in 1932 by Imperial Airways for passengers on its air route between Britain and India.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-maktoum"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-maktoum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al Maktoum</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Al Maktoum are the ruling family of Dubai, descended from Maktoum bin Butti, who led the Al Bu Falasah to Dubai Creek in 1833.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mualla"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-mualla.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al Mualla</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Al Mualla are the ruling family of Umm al-Quwain, leaders of the Al Ali tribe.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-naim"><span class="enc-name">Al Na'im</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Al Na'im are an Arab tribe of the eastern Arabian peninsula, found in the emirate of Ajman, the Buraimi oasis and northern Oman.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-nahyan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-nahyan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al Nahyan</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Al Nahyan are the ruling family of Abu Dhabi, a branch of the Al Bu Falah section of the Bani Yas tribal confederation.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-qasimi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-qasimi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al Qasimi</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Al Qasimi, or Qawasim, are an Arab dynasty and tribal confederation of the southern Gulf, centred on Ras al-Khaimah and Sharjah.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-sabah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-sabah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al Sabah</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">Al Sabah is the ruling family of Kuwait, descended from the Bani Utbah clan that settled there in the early 18th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ala-ad-din-muhammad-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ala-ad-din-muhammad-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ala ad-Din Muhammad II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ala ad-Din Muhammad II was the shah of the Khwarazmian Empire from 1200 to 1220.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ala-ad-din-tekish"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ala-ad-din-tekish.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ala ad-Din Tekish</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ala ad-Din Tekish was the ruler of Khwarazm from 1172 to 1200.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ala-al-dawla-bozkurt"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ala-al-dawla-bozkurt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ala al-Dawla Bozkurt</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ala al-Dawla Bozkurt was bey of the Dulkadirids, the Turkmen principality around Elbistan and Maraş, from 1480 to 1515.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ala-al-dawla-muhammad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ala-al-dawla-muhammad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ala al-Dawla Muhammad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ala al-Dawla Muhammad, known as Ibn Kakuya, was the founder of the Kakuyid dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ala-al-din-ali"><span class="enc-name">Ala al-Din Ali</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ala al-Din Ali was the last effective ruler of the Eretnid sultanate of central Anatolia, grandson of its founder Eretna.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ala-al-din-husayn"><span class="enc-name">Ala al-Din Husayn</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ala al-Din Husayn was ruler of Ghor from about 1149 to 1161.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-ala-ibn-mughith"><span class="enc-name">Al-Ala ibn Mughith</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Ala ibn Mughith was an Arab leader in al-Andalus who in 763 raised the black banner of the Abbasid caliph al-Mansur against the Umayyad emir Abd al-Rahman I…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ala-singh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ala-singh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ala Singh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ala Singh was a Sikh chief of the Phulkian family and the founder of Patiala State.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ala-ud-din-ahmad-shah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ala-ud-din-ahmad-shah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ala-ud-Din Ahmad Shah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ala-ud-Din Ahmad Shah was a sultan of the Bahmani Sultanate in the Deccan, reigning from 1436 to 1458 at Bidar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ala-ud-din-bahman-shah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ala-ud-din-bahman-shah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ala-ud-Din Bahman Shah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ala-ud-Din Bahman Shah, born Hasan, was the founder of the Bahmani Sultanate in the Deccan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/alaafin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/alaafin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Alaafin</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Alaafin was the king of the Oyo Empire, a sacred ruler regarded as the embodiment of divine Yoruba kingship.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/alabama"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/alabama.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Alabama</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Alabama is a state in the south-eastern United States, stretching from the Tennessee River valley to the Gulf of Mexico at Mobile.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/alaeddin-bey"><span class="enc-name">Alaeddin Bey</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Alaeddin Bey was ruler of the Karamanids in central Anatolia from about 1361.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/alaeddin-mosque"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/alaeddin-mosque.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Alaeddin Mosque</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Alaeddin Mosque is the main mosque of the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum in Konya, Turkey, standing on Alaeddin Hill at the heart of the city.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/alagakkonara-family"><span class="enc-name">Alagakkonara family</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Alagakkonara, or Alakeshvara, were a powerful family of ministers in 14th- and early 15th-century Sri Lanka, of south Indian origin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/alahis"><span class="enc-name">Alahis</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Alahis was a Lombard Duke of Trent, and later of Brescia, who rebelled against King Cunipert.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/alai-darwaza"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/alai-darwaza.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Alai Darwaza</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Alai Darwaza is a domed gateway in the Qutb Minar complex in Delhi, built in 1311 by Alauddin Khalji as the southern entrance to the Quwwat-ul-Islam mosque.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/alain-bouchart"><span class="enc-name">Alain Bouchart</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Alain Bouchart was a Breton lawyer and chronicler who served Duke Francis II and Duchess Anne of Brittany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/alain-leroy-locke"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/alain-leroy-locke.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Alain LeRoy Locke</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Alain LeRoy Locke was an American writer, philosopher and educator, known as the philosophical architect, or Dean, of the Harlem Renaissance.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/alake-of-egbaland"><span class="enc-name">Alake of Egbaland</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Alake of Egbaland is the paramount traditional ruler of the Egba, a Yoruba people whose capital is Abeokuta in south-western Nigeria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/alaknanda"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/alaknanda.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Alaknanda</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Alaknanda is a Himalayan river in Uttarakhand, India, and one of the two headstreams of the Ganges.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/alaksandu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/alaksandu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Alaksandu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Alaksandu was a king of Wilusa in north-western Anatolia in the early 13th century BCE, known from a treaty he made with the Hittite king Muwatalli II around 1280 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/alalakh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/alalakh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Alalakh</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Alalakh is a Bronze Age city mound at Tell Atchana near Antakya in southern Turkey, in the Amuq plain.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/alalia"><span class="enc-name">Alalia</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Alalia was a Greek colony on the east coast of Corsica, founded by Phocaeans around 565 BCE, on the site of modern Aléria.</span></a></li></ul>
